1
00:00:00,595 --> 00:00:07,193
AI models repeat prices, versions, laws and job titles long after they change.

2
00:00:07,193 --> 00:00:12,159
This is the Stale-Answer Detector, for challenge five of the Geek Room Global AI

3
00:00:12,159 --> 00:00:18,818
Hackathon. It re-checks an AI answer against the live web, using Bifrost and Tavily.

4
00:00:21,162 --> 00:00:24,169
First, let's get a stale answer.

5
00:00:24,169 --> 00:00:28,987
The ask command sends a question through the Bifrost gateway to a deliberately older

6
00:00:28,987 --> 00:00:38,305
model, Llama 3.1 8B. It says Parag Agrawal runs Twitter, the latest Python is 3.9.9,

7
00:00:38,305 --> 00:00:43,067
and Node 14 is the Active LTS. All three sound confident.

8
00:00:45,362 --> 00:00:51,153
Here is how the check works. Through Bifrost, a fast model splits the answer into

9
00:00:51,153 --> 00:00:55,142
claims and rates how time-sensitive each one is.

10
00:00:55,142 --> 00:00:57,346
Tavily runs two searches per claim:

11
00:00:57,346 --> 00:01:02,471
one for the current state, and one that looks for news that it changed.

12
00:01:02,471 --> 00:01:07,778
A stronger model judges each claim, and plain code verifies every quote and computes

13
00:01:07,778 --> 00:01:08,539
the confidence.

14
00:01:10,829 --> 00:01:17,029
Here is the result. Three claims, three re-checked, and all three have changed.

15
00:01:17,029 --> 00:01:22,017
Parag Agrawal was removed as CEO in 2022.

16
00:01:22,017 --> 00:01:29,540
The newest stable Python is 3.15.1, released in October 2026.

17
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:33,438
And Node 24 is the Active LTS line.

18
00:01:33,438 --> 00:01:38,540
The run took four Bifrost calls and five Tavily searches, for about a tenth of a

19
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:38,892
cent.

20
00:01:41,195 --> 00:01:45,770
Each run writes a Markdown, HTML and JSON report.

21
00:01:45,770 --> 00:01:48,986
This one checks a longer sample answer.

22
00:01:48,986 --> 00:01:53,680
Each card shows the verdict, a confidence bar, the newest evidence date,

23
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:58,816
and how many sites agree. Then the quotes, with links, and in yellow,

24
00:01:58,816 --> 00:02:02,543
the model's inference, kept apart from the evidence.

25
00:02:02,543 --> 00:02:06,168
Code checks every quote against the page text.

26
00:02:06,168 --> 00:02:08,305
An invented quote is thrown out.

27
00:02:10,595 --> 00:02:13,250
Not everything can be confirmed.

28
00:02:13,250 --> 00:02:17,784
The sample answer says a Pune robotics startup, Brightwave Labs,

29
00:02:17,784 --> 00:02:20,148
raised a forty million dollar Series B.

30
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:25,420
Tavily only finds unrelated companies with similar names, so the claim is marked

31
00:02:25,420 --> 00:02:29,398
unverified, with low confidence, instead of guessed.

32
00:02:29,398 --> 00:02:33,943
At the bottom, the rewrite step produces a corrected answer, with numbered

33
00:02:33,943 --> 00:02:34,693
citations.

34
00:02:36,995 --> 00:02:43,048
Now, reliability. With simulate outage, the primary model points at a provider that

35
00:02:43,048 --> 00:02:48,934
is down. Bifrost answers from the fallback models, the summary warns which models

36
00:02:48,934 --> 00:02:51,730
served the requests, and the check still completes.

37
00:02:57,195 --> 00:03:02,355
The app only holds a Bifrost virtual key, with a thirty cent monthly budget,

38
00:03:02,355 --> 00:03:05,623
a daily rate limit, and a model allow-list.

39
00:03:05,623 --> 00:03:13,441
No key gets a 401. A model outside the list gets a 403, before any provider is

40
00:03:13,441 --> 00:03:13,827
called.

41
00:03:16,129 --> 00:03:20,470
Twenty-six tests run offline, with Bifrost and Tavily mocked,

42
00:03:20,470 --> 00:03:24,481
covering claim selection, quote verification, and the confidence score.

43
00:03:29,929 --> 00:03:35,145
The code, setup instructions, sample runs and known limitations are all in the

44
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:38,026
GitHub repository. Thanks for watching.
